{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"dd70585c-5879-4cec-9707-9c7681b8e426","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Displace a pendulum slightly from vertical. Its angle measures displacement, and gravity pulls it back toward equilibrium.","ops":[{"op":"diagram","id":"pendulum","region":"left","primitives":[{"kind":"line","points":[[0.32,0.15],[0.68,0.15]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"fixed support"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.5,0.15],[0.5,0.82]],"stroke":"dashed","meaning":"vertical equilibrium position"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.5,0.15],[0.66,0.78]],"stroke":"solid","label":"L"},{"kind":"ellipse","center":[0.66,0.78],"radius":[0.035,0.035],"stroke":"solid","fill":true,"label":"m"},{"kind":"arc","center":[0.5,0.15],"radius":[0.2,0.2],"start_deg":76,"end_deg":90,"stroke":"solid","label":"θ"}]},{"op":"text","id":"heading","region":"C1","content":"Pendulum: small oscillations"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"dd70585c-5879-4cec-9707-9c7681b8e426","step":{"id":"s2","script":"The tangential component of gravity opposes the displacement. Dividing tangential force by mass and length gives angular acceleration.","ops":[{"op":"arrow","id":"restoring","canvas_id":"pendulum","from":[0.66,0.78],"to":[0.49,0.823],"stroke":"solid","label":"restoring"},{"op":"equation","id":"force","anchor":{"el":"heading","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"F_{\\mathrm{t}}=-mg\\sin\\theta"},{"op":"equation","id":"angular","anchor":{"el":"force","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"mL\\ddot{\\theta}=-mg\\sin\\theta"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.warning","request_id":"dd70585c-5879-4cec-9707-9c7681b8e426","code":"step_repaired","step_hint":"s3"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"dd70585c-5879-4cec-9707-9c7681b8e426","step":{"id":"s3","script":"For small angles measured in radians, sine theta is approximately theta. Acceleration then becomes proportional to displacement and opposite in direction: simple harmonic motion.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"approx","anchor":{"el":"angular","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"\\sin\\theta\\approx\\theta\\quad (|\\theta|\\text{ small, in radians})"},{"op":"equation","id":"shm","anchor":{"el":"approx","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"\\ddot{\\theta}=-\\frac{g}{L}\\theta=-\\omega^2\\theta"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If the pendulum is displaced to the right, which way does its restoring acceleration point?","expected_gist":"Back toward equilibrium, to the left along the swing."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"dd70585c-5879-4cec-9707-9c7681b8e426","step":{"id":"s4","script":"The period increases with the square root of length, but does not depend on mass. This assumes small swings, negligible friction, and a light string.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"period","anchor":{"el":"shm","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"\\omega=\\sqrt{\\frac{g}{L}},\\qquad T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{g}}"},{"op":"equation","id":"scaling","anchor":{"el":"period","side":"below","gap":0.06},"latex":"L\\to4L\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad T\\to2T"}],"checkpoint":null}}
